WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.760
What do we know about witches?

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.800
The woman with
magical powers and spells,

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.480
in a pointy hat, with a broomstick
and a black cat.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.840
But behind this stereotype

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.360
is a long, dark history
of torture and persecution. 

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.360
Much of what we think we know

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.240
comes from the
16th and 17th centuries,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.440
and especially the work of the
witchfinder Matthew Hopkins.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.800
It led to the deaths

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.120
of around 100 people,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.600
and still has echoes

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:37.920
in our lives today.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.920
In the 16th and 17th Century,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.440
people in Europe were living in what
historians call a magical universe -

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.800
a realm where
it was implicitly accepted

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.600
that God and the Devil were real.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.400
Life was hard in England at the time.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.720
People were often hungry,
angry, and fearful.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.680
Most people lived in small
rural communities,

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.880
and between 1530 and 1630,
the population doubled.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.400
There were plague outbreaks,

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.480
and a “Little Ice Age”,
causing harvests to fail.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.560
Everyday misfortunes
could be interpreted

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.240
as either a punishment from God,
bad luck, or the act of a witch.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.160
It was widely believed
witches could raise storms,

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.000
destroy livestock, drive people mad,
or even kill them.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.320
 Witch trials followed legal process,

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.160
and acquittal rates were actually
relatively high.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.000
Only 22% of those tried
in South East England were executed.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.920
While burning at the stake
was common across Europe,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.360
in England, witches
were usually hanged.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.640
In the 1640s, during the chaos
of the English Civil War,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.400
the witch-hunter Matthew Hopkins
appeared on the scene,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.600
along with his associate,
John Stearne.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.400
Known as the Witchfinder General,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.080
Hopkins would ride into villages
in East Anglia,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.080
often invited by concerned citizens,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.400
and listen to their suspicions.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.000
With his assistants, he would search
for the "witch's mark"

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.880
or "teat" where familiars
were thought to suck blood.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.280
Familiars were thought
to be a witch’s companion,

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.200
half-animal, half-demon.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.320
They included toads,
ferrets and cats.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.360
He had suspect witches
watched for days,

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.800
to see if their animal familiar
would come to them.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.320
In reality, this was
sleep deprivation,

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.960
designed to break
suspects into confessing. 

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.880
Hopkins also used the swimming test.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.280
A suspected witch’s toes and thumbs
were tied together

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.120
and they were
submerged in water.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.160
It was thought a witch would float,
rejected by the pure water,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.200
but the innocent would sink.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.480
Women were considered at the time
to be more vulnerable

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.040
to temptation from the Devil. 

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.400
Some women were particularly
vulnerable to accusations,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.000
due a lack of protection
in a patriarchal society.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.040
And although most of the witches

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.720
accused during the Hopkins
witch-hunt were women, 

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.120
in other parts of Europe,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.680
greater numbers of men
were persecuted as witches,

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.520
and men were caught up in
the Hopkins witch-hunt too.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.480
The elderly clergyman, John Lowes,

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.960
was accused of witchcraft in his
town of Brandeston in Suffolk. 

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.720
He was examined by Hopkins

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.640
who "found" two teats on his head
and one beneath his tongue. 

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.120
Under torture, he confessed
to having six familiars

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.040
which he had allegedly ordered
to sink a ship, killing 14 men.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.200
Although he later retracted
this confession,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.440
on August 27, 1645, he was
hanged in Bury St Edmunds.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.480
Yet opposition to Hopkins’
witch-hunt was growing.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.240
People began to condemn his methods.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.080
His influence waned and,
after an illness,

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.200
he died at home in August 1647.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.680
Overall, around 250 people
were investigated for witchcraft

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.200
during the Hopkins witch-hunt,

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:45.160
and about 100 of these
were ultimately condemned.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.240
The Hopkins witch-hunt
was a brief and tragic period

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.400
in the history of England.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.680
Many people in the
16th and 17th centuries

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.880
believed in the
necessity of witch-hunts

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.280
to protect their communities.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.440
But these persecutions
can also be seen

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.720
as a case of blaming
vulnerable people

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.360
for the difficulties of the time.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.280
We live in a very
different world today,

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.680
but has the witch-hunt
mentality gone away? 

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.400
We still have a tendency to accept
easy answers to complex problems.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.400
Social media fuels
both conspiracy theories

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:16.840
and the public shaming of others.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.040
Fear and uncertainty
is still being used

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.640
to scapegoat vulnerable
sections of society. 

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.440
So while the context
may be different,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.920
witch-hunts, it seems,
are alive and well.

